            <ArticleTitle>Atypical presentation of axillary web syndrome (AWS) in a male squash player: a case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>294-298</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Axillary Web Syndrome (AWS), also known as lymphatic cording, refers to a condition in which a rope-like soft-tissue density develops in the axilla. It usually appears in the 5 to 8 week period following breast cancer surgery and can lead to shoulder pain and restricted motion. We present a case of AWS in a male squash player with no history of breast cancer or surgery following a period of intense exercise. This case highlights the rare presentation of AWS in a male patient and raises awareness for the health care practitioner who may not suspect this condition in this population.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
